---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PaycheckTool from '../../components/PaycheckTool.astro';
import RelatedTools from '@kit/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { SITE, toolBySlug, toolsExcept } from '../../lib/site';
import { STATE_TAX } from '@data/states-tax';

const tool = toolBySlug('paycheck-calculator')!;
const canonical = `/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const title = `Take-Home Paycheck Calculator | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'Work out your net pay after federal, state, Social Security, Medicare and ' +
  'pre-tax deductions — with every line itemised rather than summed.';

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Calculators', item: `${SITE.url}/tools` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: tool.nav, item: `${SITE.url}${canonical}` },
  ],
}];
const related = toolsExcept(tool.slug).slice(0, 3).map((t) => ({
  href: `/tools/${t.slug}`, title: t.title, desc: t.blurb,
}));
const byName = [...STATE_TAX].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema}>
  <PaycheckTool
    state={null}
    title="Take-Home Paycheck Calculator"
    intro="Gross to net, one line at a time. Most calculators lump your deductions together; this one shows which wage base each deduction actually reduces, because they are not the same and the difference is real money."
    breadcrumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Calculators' }, { label: tool.nav }]}
  >
    <section style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Paycheck calculator by state</h2>
        <p>State income tax is the single biggest difference between two identical salaries. Nine states charge none at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-row">
        {byName.map((s) => <a class="chip" href={`/paycheck-calculator/${s.slug}`}>{s.name}</a>)}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <h2>The mistake almost every paycheck calculator makes</h2>
      <p>
        Pre-tax deductions do not all reduce the same thing, and treating them
        as one bucket is the most common error in this category.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Section 125 deductions</strong> — health, dental and vision
          premiums, FSA contributions, and HSA contributions made through
          payroll — reduce your income-tax wages <em>and</em> your Social
          Security and Medicare wages.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Traditional 401(k) contributions</strong> reduce your
          income-tax wages only. Social Security and Medicare are still charged
          on every dollar you contribute.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Why it matters.</strong> If a calculator treats your 401(k) as
        reducing FICA, it overstates your take-home by 7.65% of whatever you
        contribute. On a $10,000 contribution that is $765 a year of money you
        do not actually keep. The breakdown above states which base each line
        reduces so you can check it against a real pay stub.
      </div>

      <h2>What comes out, in order</h2>
      <h3>Pre-tax deductions</h3>
      <p>
        Taken first, before any tax is calculated. This is why raising your
        401(k) contribution costs you less in take-home than the contribution
        itself — at a 22% marginal rate, $100 into the plan costs about $78 of
        net pay.
      </p>
      <h3>Federal income tax</h3>
      <p>
        Calculated on wages minus pre-tax deductions minus the standard
        deduction, then run through the bracket table. Brackets are marginal:
        moving into the 22% bracket does not tax your whole income at 22%, only
        the portion above the threshold. Your effective rate is always lower
        than your marginal rate, and this calculator shows both.
      </p>
      <h3>Social Security and Medicare</h3>
      <p>
        Social Security is 6.2% up to an annual wage base, after which it stops
        for the rest of the year. Medicare is 1.45% with no cap, plus an extra
        0.9% on wages above $200,000 for single filers or $250,000 for joint.
        Your employer pays a matching 6.2% and 1.45% that never appears on your
        stub — the true cost of employing you is roughly 7.65% above your gross.
      </p>
      <h3>State income tax</h3>
      <p>
        The biggest variable between two identical salaries. Nine states tax no
        wage income at all. Twelve apply a single flat rate. The rest run
        progressive brackets that look like the federal system but with
        different thresholds, different standard deductions, and in a dozen
        states an additional local income tax layered on top.
      </p>

      <h2>Where this model stops</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Local income tax is not calculated.</strong> A dozen states allow counties or cities to levy their own. Where that applies the page says so, and your real take-home will be lower than shown.</li>
        <li><strong>Withholding is not the same as tax owed.</strong> This models your annual liability spread evenly. Actual withholding follows IRS tables applied to each individual paycheck, so a bonus month or an uneven schedule will differ.</li>
        <li><strong>Credits are excluded.</strong> The child tax credit, earned income credit and education credits all reduce what you finally owe but are claimed on your return, not withheld from your cheque.</li>
        <li><strong>Itemised deductions are excluded.</strong> This uses the standard deduction, which roughly nine in ten filers take.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>Why is my real paycheck different from this?</dt>
        <dd>
          Usually withholding tables versus annual liability. Payroll withholds
          from each cheque as though you earned that amount all year, so
          irregular pay produces uneven withholding that evens out at filing.
          Local tax and employer-specific deductions account for most of the
          rest.
        </dd>
        <dt>Does a raise ever leave me worse off?</dt>
        <dd>
          Not through tax brackets — only the income above a threshold is taxed
          at the higher rate. It can happen through benefit cliffs, where a
          subsidy or credit phases out entirely at a given income, but the
          bracket system itself never penalises earning more.
        </dd>
        <dt>Should I contribute more to my 401(k)?</dt>
        <dd>
          Capture the full employer match first — that is an immediate return
          nothing else matches. Beyond that, drag the contribution slider and
          watch the take-home figure: the gap between what you contribute and
          what you lose from your cheque is your marginal rate working for you.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools items={related} />
  </PaycheckTool>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/paycheck-island';
</script>
